Filter class test (class/)
==========================

The 'class' test declares its OTel filters as instances of the 'otel' filter
class, the declaration mode HAProxy 3.5 and later offer beside the legacy filter
'opentelemetry' line.  It runs traffic through the enabled instance and keeps a
disabled one in place, so both states of a declared instance can be read back
from the exported telemetry and from the CLI.  A second frontend inherits its
filter instance and its group action from a named defaults section, which is
how a shared action reaches the proxy that executes it.

The declaration mode needs an executable that carries the filter class, which no
HAProxy before 3.5 does.  The test needs an origin server on 127.0.0.1:8000 and
a collector that listens for OTLP/HTTP on port 4318.


Files
-----

  class/haproxy.cfg  Frontends declaring the filter instances
  class/otel.cfg     One OTel configuration section per instance, the group
                     definition included
  class/otel.yml     Exporter, processor, reader and provider definitions


Declarations
------------

  frontend otel-test-class-frontend
    ...
    filter-config otel id otel-test-class enabled config class/otel.cfg
    filter-config otel id otel-test-class-off config class/otel.cfg

The instance is disabled unless the word 'enabled' follows its id, so the first
line attaches the filter to the streams of the frontend.  The second one is
parsed, checked and initialized like the first, attaches to no stream, and reads
the top-level OTel scope named after its own filter id; one configuration file
therefore serves both.

The CLI lists the instances in the order they were declared, and a disabled
one reports its state:

  request: otel/otel-test-class > otel/otel-test-class-off

  class:      otel
  id:         otel-test-class-off
  state:      disabled
  sides:      global request response
  defined at: class/haproxy.cfg:18
  args:       config class/otel.cfg

A named defaults section declares one more instance, inherited by the second
frontend together with a group action:

  defaults otel-test-class-defaults
    filter-config otel id otel-test-class-inherit enabled config class/otel.cfg
    http-request otel-group otel-test-class-inherit otel-test-class-group

  frontend otel-test-class-inherit-frontend
    bind *:10081

The action belongs to the section but runs in the proxy that inherits it, so it
resolves against the filter configuration of that proxy.  The CLI names the
inherited copy as such, and reports the site of the declaration, which is the
line of the section:

  request: otel/otel-test-class-inherit

  class:      otel
  id:         otel-test-class-inherit
  state:      enabled, inherited
  sides:      global request response
  defined at: class/haproxy.cfg:33
  args:       config class/otel.cfg


Events
------

  Instance                   Scope             Event
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  otel-test-class            class-request     on-frontend-http-request
  otel-test-class-off        class-off-request on-frontend-http-request
  otel-test-class-inherit    inherit-scope     (none)

The enabled instance opens a root span named 'class enabled request' on the
frontend HTTP request.  The scope of the disabled instance keeps its own name,
'class disabled request', but the instance attaches to no stream, so its span is
never opened; the scope is checked like any other, so a mistake in it still
fails the load.

The scope of the inherited instance binds no event, so no event of its own runs
it; the group action the defaults section declares is what runs it, and the span
it opens is named 'inherited group request'.  A span exported by the inheriting
frontend therefore proves that the action reached the filter instance of the
proxy that inherited it, rather than that an event reached the scope.


Running the test
----------------

The run-class.sh script requires the HAProxy executable path and starts one
HAProxy instance with haproxy-common.cfg and class/haproxy.cfg after checking
that the executable carries a filter class:

  % ./run-class.sh ../../haproxy/haproxy
  % ./run-class.sh --help

When no arguments are given, the runner prints help to standard error and exits
with EX_USAGE (64) without starting HAProxy.  Explicit '-h' or '--help' prints
help to standard output and exits 0.

Optional positional arguments select the PID file and log file, as in the other
scenario runners.  A missing or non-executable binary path exits with EX_NOINPUT
(66).  An executable that carries no filter class, or whose build options cannot
be read at all, exits with EX_UNAVAILABLE (69).

Send a request to each frontend and read what the OTLP/HTTP recorder captured:

  % curl http://127.0.0.1:10080/
  % curl http://127.0.0.1:10081/

Only 'class enabled request' spans are exported for the first frontend, one per
request, whatever the traffic; the disabled instance attaches to no stream and
exports nothing.  The second frontend exports one 'inherited group request' span
per request, opened by the group action its defaults section declares.  Stop the
instance with 'kill -USR1' so the exporters flush what they still hold.
